Very little by itself. If Greg normally pauses before answering, a pause is his **baseline**, not evidence that he’s inventing—or retrieving—a prepared story.

A ready explanation could still come after a pause because he’s choosing precise wording. An unprepared answer could also come quickly. More informative than timing is what the answer actually does:

- directly explains why the tips were moved and under whose authority;
- gives details that remain consistent when discussed naturally;
- or instead avoids the transaction, asks how you found out, or pivots to your motives.

Even those patterns aren’t proof of dishonesty. His pause mainly tells you to let the silence sit rather than interpreting it in the moment.
